    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I just finished a rumenotomy on one of my dairy cows. She's stable for now but I'm a bit worried about the recovery process. Can you give me a rundown of what I should be looking out for over the next few days, especially regarding infection and when I can start getting her back on regular feed?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're welcome to reach out anytime for health updates. I can definitely guide you through the recovery process. Post-rumenotomy, it’s crucial to monitor for signs of infection such as unusual discharge, increased heart rate, or reduced appetite. Maintaining the calf's proper position and keeping it warm will help in the healing process. Initiating regular feed after 24-48 hours, depending on comfort level, is advisable, but reassess with gradual increments. Ensure fresh, clean water is available, and watch her closely, especially overnight. If she shows no signs of complications after a few days, then you can slowly reintroduce solid feed. Remember to consult with your vet for a complete recovery timeline and additional symptoms to look out for. Keep me updated on her progress. Rest and care are paramount at this stage. Good luck with your cow!",
      "source": "target",
